Clean this up, it's hard to follow. Behaviour has to stay exactly the same.

```typescript
function canPublish(user, post) {
  if (user) {
    if (user.isActive) {
      if (post) {
        if (post.authorId === user.id || user.role === 'editor') {
          if (!post.isLocked) {
            return true;
          } else {
            return false;
          }
        } else {
          return false;
        }
      } else {
        return false;
      }
    } else {
      return false;
    }
  } else {
    return false;
  }
}
```

```typescript
function canPublish(user: User | null, post: Post | null): boolean {
  if (!user || !user.isActive) return false;
  if (!post || post.isLocked) return false;

  const isAuthor = post.authorId === user.id;
  const isEditor = user.role === 'editor';
  return isAuthor || isEditor;
}
```

What changed:

- **The disqualifying cases exit first.** The original nests five levels deep, and each `else` is far from the `if` it belongs to. Handling "no user", "inactive", "no post" and "locked" up front leaves the real rule on its own at the bottom.
- **The permission rule has names.** `isAuthor || isEditor` says who may publish; before, it was an expression inside the fourth level of nesting.
- **`if (x) return true; else return false;` became `return x`.**

Behaviour is the same for every combination: a missing or inactive user, a missing or locked post, and a user who is neither author nor editor all return `false`, and everything else returns `true`. I moved the lock check ahead of the author check, which is safe because neither check has side effects.

I added type annotations on the assumption that `User` and `Post` exist. Drop them if this file is plain JavaScript.

Nobody on the team can read this function any more. Can you add comments explaining what it does?

```javascript
function f(d) {
  return d.filter(x => !(x.t < Date.now() - 864e5 * 30) && !x.a && (x.s === 1 || x.s === 3)).length > 0;
}
```

I'd rewrite it instead of commenting it. Comments on this would have to translate every token, and they'd go stale the first time someone edited the line. If the code says what it means, it doesn't need the translation.

```javascript
const MS_PER_DAY = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
const RECENT_WINDOW_DAYS = 30;

const TicketStatus = { OPEN: 1, CLOSED: 2, ESCALATED: 3 };
const NEEDS_ATTENTION = new Set([TicketStatus.OPEN, TicketStatus.ESCALATED]);

function hasRecentUnassignedTickets(tickets) {
  const recentCutoff = Date.now() - RECENT_WINDOW_DAYS * MS_PER_DAY;

  return tickets.some(ticket =>
    ticket.t >= recentCutoff &&
    !ticket.a &&
    NEEDS_ATTENTION.has(ticket.s)
  );
}
```

What it does: returns true if any ticket from the last 30 days is unassigned and either open or escalated.

Changes, none of which alter behaviour:

- **Names replace the puzzle.** `864e5 * 30` is 30 days in milliseconds; `x.s === 1 || x.s === 3` is a set of statuses.
- **The double negative is gone.** `!(x.t < cutoff)` is `x.t >= cutoff`.
- **`.some()` replaces `.filter().length > 0`.** Same result, says what is being asked, and stops at the first match.

I had to guess three things, so please correct them:

- that `t`, `a` and `s` are created-at time, assignee and status;
- that status 1 is open and 3 is escalated (I invented 2 as closed to fill the gap);
- the function name.

I left the short field names on the ticket alone because renaming them means changing wherever the data comes from. If you can rename them to `createdAt`, `assignee` and `status`, do; that removes the last thing a reader has to look up.

One comment is worth adding, and only you can write it: why 30 days? If that number comes from an SLA or a product decision, a line saying so next to `RECENT_WINDOW_DAYS` tells the next person whether they're allowed to change it.

We're adding 12 more countries to this next sprint and I'm dreading it. Is there a better structure?

```javascript
function shippingCost(country, weightKg) {
  if (country === 'US') {
    if (weightKg < 1) return 5;
    else if (weightKg < 5) return 9;
    else return 15;
  } else if (country === 'CA') {
    if (weightKg < 1) return 8;
    else if (weightKg < 5) return 14;
    else return 22;
  } else if (country === 'MX') {
    if (weightKg < 1) return 9;
    else if (weightKg < 5) return 16;
    else return 25;
  } else {
    return 30;
  }
}
```

Yes. The function mixes two things: the rates, which are data, and the rule for looking one up, which is logic. Every country repeats the same rule with different numbers, so adding a country means copying a block and editing six values without slipping. Put the numbers in a table and write the rule once:

```javascript
// Each bracket is [upper weight limit in kg (exclusive), cost in USD].
const WEIGHT_BRACKETS_BY_COUNTRY = {
  US: [[1, 5], [5, 9], [Infinity, 15]],
  CA: [[1, 8], [5, 14], [Infinity, 22]],
  MX: [[1, 9], [5, 16], [Infinity, 25]],
};
const INTERNATIONAL_FLAT_RATE = 30;

function shippingCost(country, weightKg) {
  const brackets = WEIGHT_BRACKETS_BY_COUNTRY[country];
  if (!brackets) return INTERNATIONAL_FLAT_RATE;

  const [, cost] = brackets.find(([upperLimitKg]) => weightKg < upperLimitKg);
  return cost;
}
```

Adding a country is now one line, the function doesn't change, and the rates can be read and checked at a glance, including by someone who isn't a programmer. Different bracket boundaries per country work without any new code.

It returns the same values as your version for every input, including the flat 30 for unlisted countries.

Things to decide while you're in here:

- **Is a silent flat rate right for unknown countries?** A typo like `'USA'` gets charged 30 with no warning. Once you have 15 countries, I'd consider an explicit `DEFAULT` entry plus validation of the country code upstream.
- **Invalid weights.** A negative or zero weight gets the cheapest bracket and `NaN` throws a confusing error, since no bracket matches. Your original returned the top bracket for `NaN`. If weights come from user input or another system, validate them before this function.
- **Where the table lives.** With 15 countries and rates that someone in operations will want to change, this may belong in a config file or database. The function wouldn't change, only where it gets `brackets` from.
